WEBVTT

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:45.240
There's nowhere I'm more at home than in our stunning British countryside.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:50.840
From coastlines to castles, and from boats to bridges, I've pretty much seen it all.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:59.640
I've explored almost every corner of rural Britain and discovered fascinating stories about its people and history.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:09.160
It's all been a wonderful experience for me over the years and today I am celebrating my 25th anniversary on Countryfile.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:14.520
It was in July, 1989 that I made my very first appearance on the programme.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:19.520
Since then, the show has undergone many changes, and so has the countryside.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:29.440
To mark the occasion, and for one week only, I've been asked to step into the editor's shoes, so it is up to me what's going to be in the programme.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.480
I've sent Ellie on a wildlife mission.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.320
Oh, wow!

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.600
She's looking right at me.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:40.600
I've been rumbled!

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.840
I've asked Tom to look into the latest state of play with organics.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.360
They are a fantastic colour.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.840
They really shine out, don't they?

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:55.000
Matt is driving along a country road I know that helped change motoring history.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:00.840
If there was a list of ways to experience the British countryside, this would be at the top.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.840
And I'll be celebrating on Adam's farm, with my old friend.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.360
Whoa!

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.960
..Another 25 years!

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.800
Well, I'm not sure about THAT!

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:35.480
'I've come to the Countryfile base at BBC Bristol 'for my first task as guest editor.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:39.200
'I've got to decide, with the help of the production team, 'what to put into the programme.'

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.440
Guys, a nice day to be outside.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.960
Morning, everybody.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.440
This is the first time I've met all of you around a conference table.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.040
Normally it's in the middle of a field somewhere.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:54.640
So, it's the first time I've been in an editor's chair since my Newsround days!

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.240
Brilliant, how does it feel, John?

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.320
I don't know, I'll tell you later!

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:04.840
But I suppose an awful lot of you weren't even born when I started doing Countryfile, a quarter of a century ago.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.720
Afraid so!

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.400
These are just some of the names that you will see on the credits at the end of the programme.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:20.200
We've got researchers, directors, runners, the production management team and, of course, producers.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:27.960
What I'd like to do on this programme is look back at some of the big issues we've dealt with over those years, and some of the fun times, as well.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.440
Anybody remember anything in particular?

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:34.840
I remember seeing a piece on the Isle of Man when you were driving around in a motorcycle, and you looked like you were loving it.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.360
Yes!

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.400
On a BSA Bantam.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:46.320
When I was a boy, I used to go there, to the TT races, on my little Bantam, and the programme managed to find one in a museum, and I had a lovely time, yes.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:54.600
I remember the very first time, watching Countryfile, and being impassioned by a story I saw there about dolphins washing up on the beach in Cornwall.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:04:02.480
Obviously, wildlife has always been a big interest of mine from my Newsround days, obviously, and then through into Countryfile.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:14.480
I think it would nice to take a look at the state especially of endangered species in this country, how are they faring now compared to 25 years ago?

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:15.720
Definitely.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.079
Here's Tom.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.600
Hi, Tom.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:22.519
'He's in today to record his commentary 'for another edition of Countryfile.'

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:23.880
And I've got a job for you later on.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.360
Have you?

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:25.800
Yes.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.760
What's that?

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:27.920
I'll tell you later.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:32.760
'I'll be briefing Ellie and Matt, as well.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.320
'And, of course, I mustn't forget Adam.'

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:49.960
Adam hasn't been part of the Countryfile family for quite as long as I have, but it is hard to imagine the programme now without his farm in the Cotswolds.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.280
If we just come back with Archie...

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.680
I've popped in to see him on one of his filming days.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:57.600
This is Archie, my new Highland bull that I'm very proud of.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.920
I bought him from the Queen...

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:06.080
It looks like being a typical day down on Adam's farm, the only one in the country with a resident film crew.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.200
Hi, John, how are you?

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.800
Good to see you!

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:10.280
And this is the famous Archie!

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.640
It is, indeed.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:10.920
How's he settling in?

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:14.200
He's all right, still a little bit lively.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.080
We're going to put him in with the cows soon.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.800
You must have seen hundreds of animals on thousands of farms, John?

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.360
I've lost count, Adam.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:26.120
In the early days, it was much more of a farming programme.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:26.440
Half an hour on Sunday mornings.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:29.920
And a much harder programme which looked at a lot of farming issues.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:37.440
But what really struck a chord with me when I first started on Countryfile, looking back, was you telling the farmers about the story of foot and mouth.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:41.280
It was a devastating time for the livestock industry.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:47.200
'The foot and mouth crisis of 2001 affected everyone, not just farmers.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:54.320
'Millions of animals were slaughtered 'and a huge sadness fell across the countryside.'

00:05:53.480 --> 00:06:00.920
'I'll never forget what I saw, heard, and smelt over those weeks.'

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:06.040
But some good eventually did come out of bad.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:13.000
Ten years after the all-clear, I visited the disused airfield in North Cumbria where half a million animals were buried.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:26.200
Now, it's an impressive nature reserve and helping me plant a commemorative tree was retired brigadier Alex Burtwhistle - the man who'd been given the task of burying all those carcasses.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:30.640
This is a much smaller hole than the ones I'm accustomed to digging here.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:38.880
But I am delighted, all jocularity aside, I'm delighted that something positive has come out of what was a very harrowing time for all concerned.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:43.000
I'm very pleased to be here with John to plant this sturdy English oak.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:52.880
I hope I haven't been an unlucky mascot for farming, because when I joined the show suddenly agriculture seemed to have a lot of diseases.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.840
Now, then, John, I know you like to get involved, so why don't you take Archie for a walk?

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.240
Yeah.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:56.680
I'm not sure I should.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.880
Remember what happened last time I handled one of your big beasts?

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.600
I remember, knocked you over, didn't he?

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.200
Come on, come on.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.280
He's behaving well.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.400
Look at that, a natural!

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:04.960
Well done.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:15.400
'They say, "Never work with children or animals".

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:16.600
'But that pretty much sums up my career in broadcasting!'

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:17.080
COW BREAKS WIND Oh!

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:25.440
'Countryfile has given me the perfect opportunity 'to indulge my passion for wildlife.'

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:32.480
'I've been able to report on many remarkable stories.'

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:39.280
We've just spotted a humpback whale, one of the great creatures of the sea.

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.240
I just caught a glimpse of it as it went down, there it is, over there.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:49.280
'Red kites were close to extinction in the UK when I first filmed them.'

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:50.240
This is the airport at Madrid, the Spanish capital.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:59.120
I'm just boarding a flight to London escorting an unusual passenger - this rare and beautiful bird of prey, a red kite.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:04.640
It's flying to London the easy way to make its home in the wild in the British countryside.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:08.240
Would you like to let the first four go now?

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:15.120
'I've been thrilled to see them go full circle, 'thriving again in the wild.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:18.000
'There are now thousands of them.'

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.280
'And I've got up close to lots of other animals also, 'like this little dormouse.'

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:23.960
There he goes.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.920
Very agile, aren't they?

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:31.320
Very much so!

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:37.039
'Working on Countryfile has given me 'privileged access to places most people can't get to.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:48.120
'But anybody can visit the Isle of Mull and see a wildlife treat - 'the nest of one of our largest birds of prey, the white tailed sea eagle.'

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:53.560
This must be the only place in the world where you can come and publicly see such a rare bird.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:57.320
It's the only place with a live nest that you can see the chicks with your own eyes.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.160
We have got our CCTV pictures, as well, you can get a close-up view.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.800
But, yeah, it's absolutely fantastic and lots of people have a very good time coming to see them.

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:16.760
As editor for this week, what I'd like to do now is to take a look at the current state of British wildlife.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.400
Are things better now than they were 25 years ago?

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:23.760
What have been the success stories and which species are in trouble?

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.360
I've asked a fellow wildlife lover, Ellie, to take a look.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:36.880
This mission is right up my street because I also have a passion for the wildlife of Britain.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:44.800
Since John has been presenting Countryfile, there have undoubtedly been some huge losses in British wildlife.

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:51.080
But it's not all bad news - there's also been some remarkable recoveries.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:55.200
Conservation scientist Mark Eaton gives me an overview.

00:09:55.280 --> 00:10:01.800
Mark, what is the general state of nature in Britain today?

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.560
Our state of nature report last year found that more species are decreasing than increasing.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:07.640
We found about 60% of the species have declined.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:12.680
The other thing we found was a lot of species showing really quite large changes.

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:17.040
So, either plummeting down, or big increases.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:19.560
But one of the good pieces of news is that we have got better at conservation, I think.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:20.640
We've seen wonderful recoveries.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:22.280
We've seen the red kite coming back.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:28.720
We've seen large blue butterflies, which were completely extinct, now flourishing in the West Country.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:32.760
So, things like that, excellent news.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:39.200
What we're less good at is animals and plants in the wider countryside which you can't help by creating a nature reserve.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:41.640
How important is farmland in this?

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:42.480
Extremely important.

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:46.520
About 75% of the UK is farmland.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:53.080
How we manage that, how we extract that food, has a huge impact on the wildlife trying to live there.

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:58.120
We need to help farmers and those who manage land to help wildlife.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:05.160
Many once-common farmland birds are on the brink of extinction in Britain.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:13.560
The turtle dove is now our fastest declining bird - in the last 25 years, it's suffered a 91% population dive.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:23.400
A lack of seed is thought to be the major cause.

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:27.560
Turtle dove expert Simon Tonkin explains the problem.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.000
Why this big decline in turtle dove numbers?

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:37.640
Essentially, one of the major concerns is finding enough food during the breeding season.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:39.240
What about this crop here, we've got wheat growing.

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:39.480
Is this no good for them?

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:45.240
Wheat's going to be available as a seed source at the back end of the season, so August time.

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:50.520
That's actually when turtle doves think about migrating back to their wintering grounds.

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:53.960
And they're a bird that just eats seeds.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:58.560
And lots of these crops are grown with very little room for wildlife, to be fair.

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:02.280
But farmers are doing their bit to help.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.840
So, Simon, this is part of the solution.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:11.320
The farmers here are putting aside at least 10% of their land to specific wildlife measures.

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:25.520
So things like this clover, birdsfoot trefoils and other things, those will set seeds at the right time of the year for when turtle doves not only arrive back here, but also when they've got young, as well.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:31.440
Boosting turtle dove numbers is not only about increasing their food.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:36.480
It's also about monitoring the behaviour of the few birds we have left.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:45.360
'I'm off on a mission with scientist Jenny Dunn, who's been tagging 'and radio tracking these extremely rare birds.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.120
'But will we track down this elusive creature?'

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:53.160
So there's a receiver on the roof to pick up the sound.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:53.880
That's right.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.360
What number is the bird?

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:57.640
This one is 951.

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:01.800
DEVICE BLEEPS Oh, is that it?

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:02.520
Yeah, that's it.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:02.680
That is.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:03.840
Yeah!

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:08.120
We're incredibly lucky to get a signal.

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:12.920
Turtle doves are so scarce it can be like looking for a needle in a haystack.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:17.960
Been monitoring this bird for a couple of weeks now, so we know it is on a nest nearby.

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:18.280
Sounds like we're getting close.

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:21.080
Yes, we're not far off now.

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:27.160
'Jenny has seen turtle doves in this spot before, 'but will they be here today?'

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:30.280
Can you see the chicks?

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:31.160
There's two, two little heads.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:32.960
Oh, wow!

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:36.280
She's looking right at me.

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:36.560
Yes, she's spotted you.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:37.800
I've been rumbled!

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:43.240
Oh, it's great.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.760
What a fantastic sight.

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:46.960
That's amazing.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:51.000
That is so fabulous.

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:52.520
They are increasingly rare.

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:58.320
I just hope that's not a sight that I'll never see again.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:58.680
That was brilliant.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:01.200
Love it.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:09.560
Whilst our turtle doves are fighting for survival, a one-time struggling species is now flourishing.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:17.200
Otters - once incredibly rare, restricted to all but a few far-flung pockets of the country.

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:23.000
Today they are in every county having made a miraculous comeback.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:29.000
'Not all our wildlife is on the brink.

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:35.640
'I'm heading to an unlikely spot for nature - 'a suburban house in a small Essex town.'

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:36.480
Hi, Sue, how are you doing?

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:41.400
Sue Manning has had a passion for otters for 13 years.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:43.840
I'm here to find out about the animals at the bottom of her garden.

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:46.960
What was it that first got you into otters?

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:53.360
Mainly because I started volunteering with the Essex Wildlife Trust.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:58.480
When you see your first otters you get quite excited because it takes a long time to find one!

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:02.360
And then you went on to start looking at them in quite a serious way.

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:08.840
Well, I knew they were here so, as soon as I put the CCTV camera up, within days I'd had my first shot.

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:09.000
Can we have a look at some of the shots?

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:09.480
Yes.

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:15.840
This was the first shot I have, and you can just see...

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:20.520
There it is.

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:20.720
Oh, yeah!

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:23.520
And that was enough to get you hooked?

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:25.160
I'm afraid so.

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:25.680
And now...

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:28.480
I see two.

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:28.960
Oh, yeah.

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:31.000
Wonderful.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:32.600
This one is quite funny.

00:15:31.640 --> 00:15:32.960
It's just...

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:36.120
Ooh!

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:38.640
That's an energetic splash.

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:38.880
That's such speed.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:39.160
Yeah.

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:39.640
Wow!

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:45.080
Have you got any footage with young?

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:45.280
Yeah, that would have been...

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:49.640
Mum, and there's babies.

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:50.520
Oh, yeah.

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:50.920
And there's another baby.

00:15:51.520 --> 00:16:00.760
And what's interesting now is that I saw Mum plus two and then, this year, Mum plus three.

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:02.760
Got to be really happy with that.

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:03.000
Yeah.

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:04.120
That's such wonderful footage.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:06.920
It's such a joy to see, right here, at the bottom of your garden.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:07.600
Yeah.

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:15.680
Otters throughout the UK are thriving, and Sue's otters are living proof of that.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:21.120
It just goes to show that conservation and habitat improvement can save wildlife.

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:29.560
If we continue these efforts, other threatened animals, like our turtle doves, should be doing as well in another 25 years.

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:35.720
There we are, John.

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:36.760
Look what we've got here.

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:38.240
The latest arrival?

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:41.400
Yes, he was born last night, a little donkey foal.

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:41.720
Is this proud Mum?

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:43.360
It is indeed.

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:49.080
I remember when you were a new arrival on Countryfile, when I met you here for the first time on this farm.

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:53.160
That was great, when you came and told me I had won the competition to have a go at presenting on the show.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:58.320
That was just amazing for me, and wonderful to meet you, and incredible news.

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:12.319
Thousands of people have sent in videotapes of themselves and from those we selected around 20 for screen testing, and then we showed those tapes to a sample of Countryfile viewers, and this is who they liked best - Adam Henson.

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:13.560
Congratulations, Adam.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.040
You're obviously from a rural background.

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:18.560
Indeed.

00:17:17.599 --> 00:17:21.880
I was very fortunate in being brought up in the Cotswolds, and born and bred on a farm.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:27.200
And now I run this with my old college friend as part of the whole business.

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:30.920
We're surrounded by Highland cattle.

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:31.760
Are they friendly?

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:32.480
Oh, yes.

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:34.640
They meet the public all the time and this one's a bit of a favourite.

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:35.040
They're no trouble at all.

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:36.840
And look what happened to you!

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:37.240
I know.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:38.640
13 years on, I'm still here.

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:42.840
Now, how did YOU get started on Countryfile?

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:53.240
Well, I'd just left Newsround, I'd been doing that for a long time, and I was looking for something different and the BBC said, "We've got this new rural affairs programme called Countryfile.

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:53.960
"Do you fancy giving that a go?"

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:56.440
So I said, "Why not?"

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:03.560
In your time on the programme the programme has changed a lot, but the countryside has changed a great deal, too.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:04.800
It certainly has, and not just farming.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:23.960
The whole sort of social issues of the countryside have gone much higher up the national agenda, and people are now very concerned about isolation, about lack of rural bus services, lack of shops and pubs, lack of affordable housing for young people - who's going to do the farming, work the countryside if they can't afford to live there?

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:26.360
All those sort of things, very important these days.

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:27.560
One thing you covered a lot was organics.

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:30.160
That's right.

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:33.480
Organics has been a huge movement, hasn't it, over the last 25 years?

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:38.040
In fact, the very first film I made for Countryfile was about organics, and does it have a future?

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:46.920
Who would have guessed just 12 months ago that the methods used by British farmers would have been questioned in quite the way they have been by the customers?

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:50.040
The green revolution has reached the fields of Britain.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:54.640
So how is the organic industry faring 25 years on?

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:56.600
I've asked Tom to investigate.

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:10.320
Organic food - for its supporters, it's better for the environment and better for us, and that's a message its devotees have been pushing for well over a quarter of a century.

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:20.080
More and more, the consumers are wanting food that's grown in this kind of soil, that hasn't seen a drop of chemical of any kind for many, many years.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:32.880
Since John first reported on the subject in 1989, one of the biggest bones of contention has been the claim organic food is healthier than conventional produce.

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:43.320
Now, in the largest study of its kind, scientists claim to be able to show a definite difference that may prove that organics are better for you.

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:54.280
The study was done by an international team funded by European money and organic supporters, the Sheepdrove Trust.

00:19:53.480 --> 00:20:00.080
Its findings are based on the results of hundreds of experiments carried out by scientists all over the world, as well as here in the UK.

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:13.200
This is Nafferton Farm, Newcastle University's Agricultural Research Unit, where they grow plots of different crops side by side, some grown conventionally, others organically, and they can see the difference.

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:20.920
Professor Carlo Leifert is one of the scientists responsible for bringing together the research to create this new analysis.

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:23.600
What were the main results from your study?

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:45.760
We found that, on average, over all crops, over all countries in the world that have carried out these sorts of studies, organic crops contained higher levels of antioxidants, lower concentrations of a toxic heavy metal called cadmium, and lower incidence of pesticide residues in organic crops.

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:49.320
What's significant, particularly, about the antioxidant findings?

00:20:48.440 --> 00:20:56.680
They have been linked to lower incidence of certain diseases in humans.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:08.480
'Carlo's claims are based on more than 340 peer-reviewed studies, 'which he says point to significant differences 'between organic and conventionally grown crops.'

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:15.000
'But, even so, he stops short of saying that organic 'definitely provides healthier food.'

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:19.360
Why are you cautious about saying that organics are better for you?

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:21.840
Well, I'm a scientist.

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:32.680
You have to now do the obvious experiment, which is compare people eating an organic diet with people eating a conventional diet to find out how much of a health impact you can get from that.

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:43.840
Although it's not proven yet, higher levels of some antioxidants have been linked with health benefits in food.

00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:46.880
Here is your typical recommended five-a-day.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:22:03.960
Now, according to Carlo's claims, if this were an organic plateful, in terms of antioxidants it would be delivering an extra one to two portions, so an organic plateful of five-a-day gives you the nutrient hit of six or seven-a-day.

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:06.600
But what impact will this study have on the organic movement?

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:12.400
Certainly, when John first reported on the subject, hopes of growth were high.

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:19.560
One prediction is that by the year 2000 20% of all British agriculture will be grown organically.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:20.560
But that never happened.

00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:38.480
In fact, rather than 20%, even by its peak in 2008, it was actually grown on less than 5% of agricultural land and, as organic produce is generally more expensive, soon after the financial downturn it went into decline.

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:42.720
So, will this report change that?

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:43.480
Lord Peter Melchett is the Policy Director of the Soil Association.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:44.680
Well, it's very exciting.

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:46.440
It's taken years to do it.

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:50.720
They've had to pull together 343 different individual research projects.

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:56.760
It's a massive job, but I think the conclusions are very positive.

00:22:56.760 --> 00:23:01.600
And what impact do you think, or hope, it will have on sales?

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:08.760
The first thing I think it will do is confirm the views of people who already buy organic.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:14.840
They generally believe that organic has a better nutrient profile for fruit and veg and so on.

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:17.480
This research confirms that.

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:28.200
Then I think it will influence some people who maybe thought that the way in which you grow food, the way in which you farm, makes no difference to the quality of the food.

00:23:27.320 --> 00:23:28.880
This research chucks that myth out the window.

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:44.800
In the past, though, high-profile studies of organics have come to a very different conclusion, prompting some pretty definitive statements, with one of the most notable coming from the then Head of the Food Standards Agency.

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:53.920
They're not getting value for money in my opinion and in the opinion of the Food Standards Agency if they think they're buying extra nutritional quality or extra safety.

00:23:54.640 --> 00:24:00.160
They really do seem to have a blind spot about organics, Sir John and the FSA.

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:03.160
Actually, he said that three years ago and it's got worse.

00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:09.960
So, is Peter Melchett now in a stronger position to make eye-catching health claims?

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:20.120
What we've got now is a meta-analysis, pulling together all these hundreds of research studies from all over the world, over a long period of time.

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:22.960
It completely changes the nature of the scientific evidence that people need to look at.

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:33.240
You've been plugging organics for at least 25 years, we've been investigating for that length of time, In all that time, you're not able to say that they're better for you, for our health.

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:34.560
You can't be too impatient, Tom.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:41.920
The first step is do the work to see if how you grow food and how you farm makes a difference to the quality of the food.

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:42.560
That's been questioned up till now.

00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:47.320
After the publication of this research I don't think it can be questioned any more.

00:24:52.960 --> 00:25:01.000
So, what does the academic who's been rubbishing the health claims of organic foods all these years make of the latest study?

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:10.280
Today, Lord Krebs is the Principal of Jesus College, Oxford.

00:25:10.920 --> 00:25:16.680
This study suggests that organic farming leads to increased levels of nutritionally desirable compounds, ie, it's better for you.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:18.640
Do you agree?

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:21.680
I don't think this study shows that.

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:22.080
That's what they claim it does.

00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:41.560
No, what they're claiming is that it contains higher levels of certain compounds, like antioxidants, that may or may not have health benefits, but they themselves acknowledge that they haven't looked at the actual health benefit consequences, so simply saying it's got more in doesn't necessarily prove that it's better for you.

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:43.040
These are not just the views of Lord Krebs.

00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:50.440
Within the scientific community there has already been criticism, questioning everything from the process to the final results.

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:59.160
Do you still think people are wasting their money buying organic food?

00:25:58.480 --> 00:25:59.760
I think it's an individual choice whether you buy organic food or not.

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:11.680
Some people may think it tastes better some may think it's better for the environment, but if you're buying it on the basis that it makes you healthier, and you're paying a premium for that, then you are still wasting your money.

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:18.200
What really matters is eating a balanced diet, and it doesn't matter whether it's organic or not.

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:29.320
So, rather than putting an end to the controversy, this scientific study seems to have reinvigorated an argument that's been rumbling on for well over a quarter of a century.

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:47.560
In the often-noisy debate over organics and health, this is a significant piece of work and, while it may not trigger an organic revolution, it may well persuade some people to spend a little bit more on what they believe is good for them.

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:58.760
'Ever since the early days of Countryfile, 'I've spent a lot of time talking to farmers...'

00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:59.600
Last word with the farmer, then.

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:04.880
If we don't look after the countryside, it'll just go to wrack and ruin.

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:08.960
'..And to a whole host of other interesting people...'

00:27:08.240 --> 00:27:13.800
That's quite a combination, Andrew, grocer and funeral director.

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:18.240
Yeah, you sort of look after them when they're living and then you look after them when they pass on to the next stage.

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:23.360
'I've met a Prince, prime ministers, 'and people who grow everything from tomatoes...'

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:24.160
Mmm!

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:25.960
Never thought I'd eat an Icelandic tomato!

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:29.080
'..To olives...'

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:30.600
And can you eat these olives, then?

00:27:29.800 --> 00:27:31.160
Do you want to try?

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:32.280
They look very inviting, don't they?

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:32.760
They do.

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:37.800
They're a beautiful colour on a lovely colour tree.

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:41.760
Urgh!

00:27:42.320 --> 00:27:47.400
'I've met butchers...'

00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:48.560
'..Bakers and a sandcastle-maker.'

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:53.720
I mean, how do you do the stonework here?

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:57.560
I just scratch in the sand here, like this, then blow the surface.

00:27:56.680 --> 00:27:58.960
You mustn't suck, or you end up with a mouthful of sand!

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:09.760
'I've lazed in the meadow with Jilly Cooper... 'taken a walk with Alan Titchmarsh, 'and even met another version of myself.'

00:28:09.560 --> 00:28:20.040
Hello, again, and on this special 20th anniversary of Countryfile, what better way to surprise John Craven than with another John Craven?

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:28.520
'There's been local historians...'

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:29.680
There's a lovely story about the hairy hands.

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:40.840
As you're driving home across Dartmoor, this hairy hand comes from nowhere and grabs the steering wheel and pulls you off the road and you have a terrible accident.

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:44.200
'..Passionate environmentalists...'

00:28:43.480 --> 00:28:49.000
The environment is what we live in and we should understand it, taste it, smell it and then respect it.

00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:52.640
'..And lots and lots of Countryfile viewers.'

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:54.520
You've washed your hands, haven't you, after that dirty work?

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:56.360
Thank you.

00:28:55.480 --> 00:28:56.840
Hey, guys, you'll never guess who this is.

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:57.400
Who's this?

00:28:56.440 --> 00:29:01.400
Hello, my name is John Craven.

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:02.360
Hi, John!

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:04.240
We need a stand-in!

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:06.040
Can you do it?

00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:08.960
Are you available?

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:13.120
I always tell my colleagues I'm much better looking than he is!

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:17.280
And I must say, very nice, similar line in sweaters going on here!

00:29:16.760 --> 00:29:23.240
Honestly, I've loved every minute of it, but one of my favourite bits has to be our annual photographic competition.

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:38.160
Picture number one is Welsh Farmer On Horseback by Myra Price, aged 15 from Dyfed.

00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:45.960
'In the 23 years that the photo competition has been running, 'I've seen a vast array of breathtaking photos.'

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:52.240
And the winner, by a margin of over 1,000 votes, is Penny Hughes with her Farmyard Scene.

00:29:53.200 --> 00:29:54.280
Congratulations, Penny.

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:58.320
'And selecting the winners were some pretty perceptive judges.'

00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:00.560
What about this one, Levitation?

00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:01.800
What would you call that?

00:30:00.920 --> 00:30:04.000
It's so sweet, that.

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:04.600
I've got the winner here.

00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:05.480
This one.

00:30:05.560 --> 00:30:06.960
What do you reckon?

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:09.240
It's a...

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:13.640
A red kite... there.

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:15.600
JOHN LAUGHS Of course it is.

00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:16.560
Of course.

00:30:15.800 --> 00:30:25.200
We first turned the winning photographs into a calendar in 2000 and they've raised millions of pounds for Children in Need.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:33.800
It would be impossible to say which I think is the best picture of the hundreds of thousands that have been sent in over the 20-odd years of the competition, but this is one of my favourites.

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:40.040
It's the overall winner from 2010 by Jennifer Duncan, and it always makes me smile.

00:30:39.520 --> 00:30:46.640
I love the way that all the other puffins are watching this one to see whether he makes a mess of the landing.

00:30:55.600 --> 00:31:01.720
This week we're celebrating John Craven's 25th anniversary on Countryfile.

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:09.680
The cake's ready... the bubbles are on ice... and we've made him editor for this particular episode.

00:31:08.720 --> 00:31:17.040
Choosing what to go into the programme has been a tough job, especially when he's done so many different things and been to so many different places.

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:19.960
Here in Bedfordshire...

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:24.920
Ironbridge Gorge in Shropshire...

00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:27.840
Bakewell in the heart of the Peak District National Park...

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:28.960
Beverly Hills in California...

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:29.960
County Galway...

00:31:29.160 --> 00:31:31.400
Dartmoor...

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:32.080
Italy...

00:31:31.240 --> 00:31:32.840
The coast of South Wales...

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:35.120
Gigha in the Inner Hebrides...

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:37.600
The South Downs of West Sussex...

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:39.800
We're on the Falkland Islands...

00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:40.560
The Isle of Mull...

00:31:39.640 --> 00:31:40.960
Romania...

00:31:40.040 --> 00:31:42.000
Iceland...

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:44.640
Shropshire...

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:45.680
Cumbria...

00:31:44.840 --> 00:31:48.640
Britain's tallest mountain, Ben Nevis.

00:31:47.680 --> 00:31:49.120
We can't see a thing of it today.

00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:54.400
But, no matter what the weather, Countryfile covers the whole of the British Isles almost every week of the year.

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:03.920
And this week I'm in the Cotswolds on Adam's farm.

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:07.320
We're very lucky though, John, aren't we?

00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:14.320
As well as seeing great places, we get to go behind the scenes, and we're always learning something new, aren't we?

00:32:13.480 --> 00:32:14.520
We are.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:14.920
You go back to familiar places, but there's things you never knew about.

00:32:14.040 --> 00:32:20.840
I mean, recently, I went to Crummackdale in the Yorkshire Dales.

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:21.920
Now, I love the Dales, but I'd never heard of Crummackdale before.

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:25.160
And in Crummackdale there's these amazing stones called the Norber Erratics.

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:25.800
It's upside down geology.

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:31.880
The older stones are at the top and they're creating fantastic shapes.

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:37.600
One place I went to that was really dramatic was to the north-west coast of Scotland, just stunning up there.

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:40.840
And the Northumberland coast is one of my favourite places, as well.

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:44.720
But really, for me, there's no place like home - the Cotswold hills.

00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:47.080
In fact, this clump of trees is very special because I can see it from miles around.

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:52.720
When I'm 20-30 miles from home, I can see it, recognise it, and know I'm nearly home.

00:32:52.440 --> 00:33:06.000
Back in 2001, I did a series of films for Countryfile about classic drives in classic cars, from a Bentley to an old Morris Minor, and I went all over the countryside.

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:12.280
I remember, in Ireland, I stopped on the banks of Galway Bay and had a little sing.

00:33:11.320 --> 00:33:12.520
I remember your dulcet tones.

00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:15.160
You've got a good voice.

00:33:14.360 --> 00:33:16.320
No!

00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:17.360
How much of that was really you?

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:28.520
Bing Crosby a bit of it.

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:58.720
Now, I'm not expecting Matt to give us a song, but I'm sure he would like to make a journey across the countryside in a classic car, so that's the assignment I've set him.

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:00.480
One-take wonder.

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:01.160
Sometimes.

00:34:00.240 --> 00:34:02.120
THEY LAUGH

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:08.800
It's a story I've found from my own knowledge of the Chiltern Hills.

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:16.760
So John has sent me to Buckinghamshire, where I'm making a trip to a rather special hill, one with a classic car connection.

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:22.120
But, right now, the only vintage vehicle is my old Land Rover.

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:30.440
Back in the early 1900s, this stunning yet sleepy bit of countryside gave its name to one of Britain's most famous classic cars.

00:34:30.440 --> 00:34:37.280
The hill on which I stand is Aston Hill and the car I'm talking about is the Aston Martin.

00:34:38.440 --> 00:34:44.520
A century ago, Aston Hill was the spot for a growing sport.

00:34:44.120 --> 00:34:53.199
Hill climbing, not on foot, but in that then rare thing - an automobile.

00:34:52.360 --> 00:35:01.240
At only 800m, the track was short, but the sharp turns demanded a lot of the new drivers.

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:10.760
In 1914, a young man called Lionel Martin took part in Aston Hillclimb.

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:26.640
Now, he was so successful in this race that, when he went on to build his own cars, he combined the name of this hill with his own name - Aston Martin - and a legend in the history of British motoring was born.

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:33.760
Vintage Aston Martins are restored by hand using traditional skills.

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:37.480
It takes years to master them.

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:42.040
Foolishly, they're letting me have a go.

00:35:43.600 --> 00:35:46.720
How long is the actual apprenticeship?

00:35:46.240 --> 00:35:56.360
I've been in this for 27 years now and I'm still learning how to, you know, get various shapes and different sort of curves, and all that sort of thing.

00:35:55.400 --> 00:35:56.520
Ooh, I went a little bit too much there.

00:35:55.680 --> 00:35:57.920
Ooh.

00:35:57.120 --> 00:35:59.480
Will this be going on a car?

00:35:58.520 --> 00:35:59.960
It will?

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:00.360
All right then, OK.

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:01.400
It definitely will.

00:36:00.440 --> 00:36:02.160
Just don't tell the customer.

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:03.360
Yeah, exactly.

00:36:02.720 --> 00:36:04.040
Yeah.

00:36:03.120 --> 00:36:06.920
Anyway, if I'm working on YOUR Aston Martin, I'm ever so sorry.

00:36:08.360 --> 00:36:14.760
With so many heritage cars here, I'm like a kid in a sweet shop.

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:20.200
Head of Aston Martin Works Paul Spires shows me a real classic.

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:22.040
Now, that car on the end there, Paul, that has caught my eye.

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:23.320
Has it?

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:25.600
Let's go and have a look at it.

00:36:24.640 --> 00:36:26.000
Yes, please.

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:27.240
Oh!

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:27.840
Look at the...

00:36:26.880 --> 00:36:28.320
Oh, the door latch!

00:36:27.440 --> 00:36:32.760
The quality.

00:36:31.840 --> 00:36:38.400
So if I was going to pop my Land Rover in part exchange for something like this, how much more would I have to spend?

00:36:38.640 --> 00:36:42.080
You'd probably have to add another £500,000 to that car.

00:36:42.240 --> 00:36:45.720
HE EXHALES DEEPLY Really?

00:36:44.920 --> 00:36:53.520
Originally, it was owned by Sir Paul McCartney and, rumour has it, he started to write the lyrics to Hey Jude actually driving this car.

00:36:52.760 --> 00:36:57.080
Really?

00:36:56.400 --> 00:37:02.400
Now, would you like to take it for a little spin?

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:02.760
Would I?!

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:04.160
Paul, yes, please.

00:37:06.080 --> 00:37:08.200
ENGINE STARTS You know what?

00:37:07.240 --> 00:37:10.680
If it's good enough for Paul McCartney, it's good enough for me.

00:37:11.360 --> 00:37:13.240
Here we go.

00:37:12.400 --> 00:37:14.120
Oh, that's so wonderful!

00:37:20.720 --> 00:37:28.000
If there was a list of ways to experience the British countryside, this would be at the top.

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:39.160
But McCartney's car is just the taxi to get me back to Aston Hill, where I'm swapping the DB6 for a heritage racing car to re-run the vintage climb.

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:47.120
I'm meeting Mark Donoghue, who owns one of the earliest Aston Martins.

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:53.600
It roared up here in the 1920s but hasn't been back since.

00:37:53.000 --> 00:38:04.760
This is a 1923 Bamford and Martin Sidevalve, known as "Cloverleaf" because of the style of the body and the little seat in the back here.

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:05.400
Oh, is that the passenger there?

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:05.720
Yeah.

00:38:04.960 --> 00:38:09.720
He started the production line in 1923 and this was one of the very first cars that rolled out of that factory.

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:13.400
And what is it like then, for you, as the owner of this car now, to be here at this very spot?

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:15.640
I'm more of a custodian.

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:16.080
Right.

00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:17.200
It's an honour.

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:21.680
And to be here 90 years on is an incredible feeling.

00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:28.880
Well, an honour for you and... an honour, an absolute honour, for me because you've allowed me to have a little go of this.

00:38:28.040 --> 00:38:32.400
There's a lot of leavers and a lot of pedals...

00:38:31.760 --> 00:38:32.840
It's... ..and they're all in the wrong order!

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:33.920
It's quite a complicated thing to drive.

00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:37.240
It took me a couple of months.

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:38.840
You've got a couple of minutes to learn.

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:42.680
You'll notice that the accelerator and the brake pedal are reversed.

00:38:42.640 --> 00:38:44.040
Yes.

00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:49.320
There is also a brake lever, which operates the rear wheels, which you have to use simultaneously.

00:38:48.480 --> 00:38:51.760
The gearbox is in reverse.

00:38:51.440 --> 00:38:54.760
First is where fourth would be on a normal car, and that's just the start.

00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:55.000
THEY LAUGH Oh, dear, me!

00:38:54.400 --> 00:38:59.080
Well, I tell you what, I'll do my best and you close your eyes.

00:38:58.360 --> 00:39:01.320
That's a good idea.

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:01.560
There you go.

00:39:02.240 --> 00:39:04.080
ENGINE REVS OK, let's see if we can get this thing moving.

00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:11.760
There we go!

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:12.920
And they're off on Aston Hill!

00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:13.440
Woo-hoo!

00:39:13.080 --> 00:39:15.040
Right, I'm going to try for second!

00:39:15.440 --> 00:39:16.640
Right.

00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:17.120
Ready?!

00:39:16.160 --> 00:39:17.520
Go for it.

00:39:21.480 --> 00:39:22.680
Oh, yes!

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:25.080
Oh, you did great!

00:39:24.480 --> 00:39:25.720
Yes!

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:27.040
That was magnificent.

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:28.080
We're in second and we're going up Aston Hill.

00:39:27.120 --> 00:39:30.200
It doesn't get any better than this, Mark.

00:39:29.240 --> 00:39:32.120
No, it doesn't.

00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:38.160
'We're only doing 30mph, but it feels like 130.'

00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:44.560
Let's see if I can get up this hill.

00:39:43.600 --> 00:39:45.280
Yeah, we'll be fine.

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:45.840
Here we go!

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:46.320
Come on, come on, come on!

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:46.920
Come on, baby!

00:39:46.040 --> 00:39:47.560
Come on!

00:39:49.080 --> 00:39:50.480
Oh, yeah.

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:52.240
You're on home turf now!

00:39:51.480 --> 00:39:53.720
Oh, yes!

00:39:54.240 --> 00:39:57.120
We're going for the record!

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:57.240
We're doing really well.

00:39:57.800 --> 00:39:59.720
Let's lean in!

00:39:59.200 --> 00:40:03.080
'Not quite the same as one of John's serene drives.'

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:03.760
Yes!

00:40:04.600 --> 00:40:09.680
You can see why this was really tough to win, can't you?

00:40:14.760 --> 00:40:16.120
We're going to go all the way!

00:40:15.400 --> 00:40:18.920
Well, we made it.

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:20.960
Obviously, Mark, this was the destination then - the top of the hill.

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:22.800
The top of Aston Hill.

00:40:21.960 --> 00:40:23.360
Indeed, it was.

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:25.240
And what a view it is.

00:40:24.680 --> 00:40:25.760
Goodness me.

00:40:24.880 --> 00:40:26.000
Beautiful, huh?

00:40:26.440 --> 00:40:35.920
For me, today, to come here to the very spot where Aston Martin got its name, and to bring this back...

00:40:35.080 --> 00:40:37.080
90 years.

00:40:36.120 --> 00:40:37.400
Almost to the day.

00:40:36.920 --> 00:40:38.280
Isn't that remarkable?

00:40:37.480 --> 00:40:39.120
You did really well.

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:41.320
Oh, well, listen, thank you.

00:40:40.520 --> 00:40:41.800
And, well, thank YOU, my dear.

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:46.160
I will never, ever, ever forget that as long as I live.

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:48.800
So, John, I owe you big time.

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:49.000
Thank you.

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:50.920
He seemed to enjoy that, didn't he?

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:51.880
He did.

00:40:51.040 --> 00:40:57.600
And I know you really enjoyed your scenic drives around the countryside, but you've had lots of other adventures along the way, haven't you?

00:40:57.320 --> 00:40:59.040
I have indeed and here's just a few.

00:41:05.840 --> 00:41:07.480
Welcome...

00:41:06.520 --> 00:41:08.800
PIGS SQUEAL Ah!

00:41:13.120 --> 00:41:15.160
We all right?

00:41:18.760 --> 00:41:22.800
Riding like the wind!

00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:30.520
I'm in training for a challenge that Charlotte set me when she went snorkelling in Wales.

00:41:30.880 --> 00:41:32.360
So, now you know.

00:41:31.520 --> 00:41:34.560
Well, now then, after all that lot, I've got one more surprise for you.

00:41:40.640 --> 00:41:41.880
Now then, John...

00:41:41.280 --> 00:41:43.040
Come and have a look at this.

00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:46.360
We've got a little bit of sparkle for you.

00:41:45.480 --> 00:41:46.720
Oh, how lovely.

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:51.840
Oh, and look at that cake.

00:41:51.040 --> 00:41:52.320
"John Craven, "outstanding in his field."

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:53.240
Oh, that's fantastic.

00:41:55.040 --> 00:41:57.880
25 years.

00:41:57.040 --> 00:42:01.720
Well, I have to admit, I didn't make it, but it is from the whole Countryfile team.

00:42:01.240 --> 00:42:03.480
Well, that is super.

00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:07.160
Congratulations, John, on reaching this incredible milestone on Countryfile.

00:42:06.200 --> 00:42:07.440
Right, let's crack open the fizz.

00:42:09.720 --> 00:42:16.760
When they said, "You're taking over from John", that is the very definition of big shoes to fill.

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:20.160
Here's to another - whoa!

00:42:19.280 --> 00:42:21.760
Another 25 years!

00:42:22.320 --> 00:42:23.800
Well...

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:26.400
Not sure about THAT!

00:42:25.600 --> 00:42:29.480
John, you are just a thoroughly, thoroughly wonderful man.

00:42:29.200 --> 00:42:35.760
All that's left, John, is for you to say goodbye.

00:42:43.600 --> 00:42:48.600
Right, well, I hope you've enjoyed my little trip down memory lane - I certainly have - and see you again very soon, I hope.

00:42:53.640 --> 00:42:54.760
Hello, Edinburgh!

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:29.120
50,000 performances across one city, in one extraordinary month.

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:37.200
We get you closer to the best live comedy, theatre, dance, literature, cabaret and music, every day across the BBC.

00:43:51.560 --> 00:43:53.000
Edinburgh - a festival for everyone.
